\chapter{Tutorial: From a session to a literature overview}
\label{ch:tutorial-llm-synthesis}

[[llm-synthesis]] teaches the [[scholar llm]] subcommands as one
pipeline with a human in the loop: configure a model once through the
[[llm]] command, give the model a research context, let it classify the
session's papers with a confidence, confirm or correct its calls, and
finally have it write the kept papers up as prose.
The object of learning is that boundary between the machine's work and
the learner's: which stages cost a model call, which stages the learner
must do for the result to mean anything, and how a paid stage can be
previewed before it is paid for.

The arc has four phases:
\begin{enumerate}
\item configure (steps 1--3 set up the [[llm]] command: the CLI is
  available, a key is stored, a default model is chosen),
\item tell the model what the review is about (steps 4--5 seed a
  session and set its research context),
\item classify with a preview first (steps 6--8 run the classifier
  dry, then for real, then confirm one of its decisions by hand),
\item synthesize with a preview first (steps 9--10 do the same for the
  literature overview), and a closing question (step 11) that asks the
  learner to sort the commands by whether they spend a call.
\end{enumerate}
Two contrasts carry the lesson.
Steps 6 and 7, and again steps 9 and 10, run the \emph{same} command with
one flag removed: [[--dry-run]] prints the prompt the model would see and
stops.
Holding everything else invariant makes the flag the only thing that
varies, so the learner experiences exactly where the cost boundary lies
and what the model is actually asked.
Steps 7 and 8 then hold the \emph{paper} invariant and vary who decided:
the classifier's keep is a candidate carrying a confidence, and the same
keep entered by the learner turns it into a reviewed decision.
Seeing the status table change while the paper does not is what makes
the human-in-the-loop rule concrete rather than a slogan.

The session lives in the tutorial's sandboxed data directory, but the
[[llm]] configuration in steps 2--3 does not: keys and the default model
belong to the [[llm]] package's own configuration files and are meant to
outlive the tutorial.
The learner is told so in the step text.
Steps 7 and 10 make real model calls; the session is small (five
papers), and both steps say beforehand that they spend a call.

\section{Tutorial source}

The whole tutorial file is one tangled root chunk.
Each step lives in its own named sub-chunk so the prose between them can
describe that step's role without breaking the Markdown output.

<<[[llm-synthesis.md]]>>=
---
id: llm-synthesis
title: From a session to a literature overview
summary: Set up the llm command, then classify, confirm and synthesize a session into a literature overview.
---

<<check llm step>>

<<configure key step>>

<<default model step>>

<<seed session step>>

<<research context step>>

<<dry run classify step>>

<<classify step>>

<<confirm decision step>>

<<dry run synthesis step>>

<<synthesize step>>

<<paid calls question step>>
@

\section{Step 1: Check that the llm command is available}

Scholar does not talk to model providers itself.
It reads the configuration of Simon Willison's [[llm]] package---the
stored keys and the default model---so the [[llm]] command line is the
surface on which the learner configures Scholar's model access.
The first step therefore only asks whether that command exists.
The required pattern is the version banner, which every [[llm]] release
prints in the same shape, so the step is a presence check that does not
break when the version number changes.

The step text has to cover two installations.
Scholar's tutorial shell puts the running interpreter's directory first
on [[PATH]] (\cref{sec:tutorial-command}), which is where [[llm]] lands
when Scholar was installed with [[uv]] or with
[[pipx install --include-deps scholarcli]]; a plain [[pipx install
scholarcli]] does not expose it, and then [[pipx install llm]] is the
fix.
Saying this here, rather than in a later step, means a learner who
lacks the command stops before storing a key that Scholar could not
read.

<<check llm step>>=
# Check that the llm command is available

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)llm\s+--version\b
  - "llm, version"
hint: "The command has a version flag; if the shell cannot find it, install it with pipx first."
```

Run `llm --version`.

Scholar's LLM features use the configuration of the `llm` package:
the API keys you store and the default model you pick. So the `llm`
command is where you set that up, once.

If the shell says the command is not found: Scholar installed with
`uv` or with `pipx install --include-deps scholarcli` brings `llm`
along, but a plain `pipx install scholarcli` does not. In that case
install it separately with `pipx install llm`, then run the version
command again.
@

\section{Step 2: Store an API key}

The key is the first of two configuration values, and it is separated
from the model choice in step~3 on purpose: a key says \emph{who} pays
for the calls, a model says \emph{what} answers them, and learners who
meet the two in one command tend to conflate them.
[[llm keys set]] prompts for the key interactively, so the transcript
never contains the key itself; the required pattern anchors on the
listing command that follows, which only prints key \emph{names}.
This also makes the step idempotent: a learner who already has a key
stored runs the listing and passes.

Scholar installs the provider plugins with itself ([[llm-openai-plugin]],
[[llm-anthropic]], [[llm-gemini]], [[llm-mistral]], [[llm-azure]],
[[llm-github-copilot]], [[llm-gpt4all]] and [[llm-openai-via-codex]]), so
the learner never installs one; [[llm plugins]] lists them.
One of them changes the shape of this step: [[llm-openai-via-codex]]
reaches OpenAI models through a Codex subscription with a login flow of
its own, and needs no stored key at all.
The required pattern therefore accepts either the key listing or the
plugin listing, so a learner without any API key still passes the step
after confirming which plugins are present, and the [[llm keys set]] part
of the text is explicitly optional.

This is the one place where the tutorial changes state outside its
sandbox.
The shell's data directory keeps Scholar's sessions inside the
workspace, but the [[llm]] configuration files are the learner's own,
and a key that vanished with the tutorial would defeat the purpose.
The step text says so plainly so the learner is not surprised later.

<<configure key step>>=
# Store an API key

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)llm\s+(?:keys\s+list|plugins)\b
hint: "Store a key with the set verb if you have one (it prompts for the value), then confirm with the keys list verb; with a Codex subscription, list the plugins instead."
```

Scholar installs the `llm` provider plugins with itself: OpenAI,
Anthropic, Gemini, Mistral, Azure, GitHub Copilot, local GPT4All
models, and OpenAI through a Codex subscription. See them with
`llm plugins`; `llm models` (next step) lists the models they provide.

If you have an API key for a provider, store it, for example:

```
llm keys set openai
```

The command asks for the key on its own prompt, so it never ends up in
your shell history. Use the provider's name as `llm` knows it. If you
have stored a key before, skip straight to the check.

If you have a Codex subscription rather than an API key, you need no
`llm keys set`: the `llm-openai-via-codex` plugin has its own login
flow, described in its README, and its models then show up in
`llm models`.

Then confirm with `llm keys list`, which prints only the names of the
stored keys, or with `llm plugins` if you rely on the Codex plugin.

Unlike the sessions you create in this tutorial, a key is stored in
your real `llm` configuration on purpose: Scholar reads it there, in
this tutorial and afterwards.
@

\section{Step 3: Choose a default model}

Scholar uses whatever [[llm]] considers the default model unless a
command overrides it, so choosing that default is the last piece of
configuration.
The step asks for three commands---list the models, set one as default,
show the default---because the middle one is silent, and the learner
needs the third to see that it took effect.
The required patterns anchor on the listing and on a [[default]]
invocation followed by a token, which accepts both the setting command
and a bare check whose output is the model name.

The step text also introduces the per-command overrides, [[-m]],
[[--model-analytic]] and [[--model-writing]], but only in prose.
They contrast with the global default along one axis---scope---and
naming them here, where the default is set, lets the learner see that
the default is a fallback rather than a lock-in.
Scholar distinguishes \emph{analytic} tasks (classification, query
generation) from \emph{writing} tasks (synthesis) because a cheap model
is often good enough for the first and rarely for the second.

<<default model step>>=
# Choose a default model

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)llm\s+models\b
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)llm\s+models\s+default\s+\S+
hint: "List the models, pick one with the default verb, then run the default verb again without an argument to see it."
```

List the models your keys unlock:

```
llm models
```

Pick one as the default, for example:

```
llm models default gpt-4o-mini
```

Then run `llm models default` with no argument: it prints the model
Scholar will use from now on.

The default is a fallback, not a lock-in. Every `scholar llm` command
accepts `-m MODEL` for one run, and two narrower overrides:
`--model-analytic` for classification and query generation, and
`--model-writing` for synthesis. A small model is often fine for the
analytic tasks; the writing usually deserves a stronger one.
@

\section{Step 4: Seed a session}

Everything the model does in this tutorial acts on one session, so the
session is created up front and is then held invariant through steps
5--10.
The search is the same small query the earlier tutorials use, on the
keyless [[openalex]] provider with a limit of five, so the classifier
has little to read, the synthesis has little to cite, and the model
calls in steps 7 and 10 stay cheap.
The required patterns anchor on the [[-n]] flag with the session name
and on Scholar's own save line, which is printed regardless of output
format.

<<seed session step>>=
# Seed a session

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+search\s+.*-n\s+llm-overview\b
  - "Saved session:"
hint: "A search with -n saves its hits to a named session; keep the provider keyless and the limit small."
```

Create the session the rest of this tutorial works on:

```
scholar search "literate programming" -p openalex -l 5 -n llm-overview
```

Five papers from one provider are enough to see every stage, and they
keep the model calls later in this tutorial small.

The session lives in this tutorial's own data directory, so it will not
appear in your real session list afterwards.
@

\section{Step 5: Set the research context}

A classifier needs a criterion.
Scholar stores it as the session's \emph{research context}: a short
statement of the research question and what makes a paper relevant to
it.
The step sets the context and then reads it back with the same command
without an argument, so the learner sees that the context is part of
the session record rather than a one-off argument.
The required patterns anchor on Scholar's confirmation line and on the
read-back.

The context given here is a question rather than a topic on purpose.
The dry run in step~6 will show it inside the prompt, and a question
makes it visible that the model is asked to judge relevance \emph{to
something}, not to sort papers by subject.
The same context is what [[scholar rq]] would have recorded had the
session started from a research question; the tutorial mentions that
shortcut at the end, once the learner has seen what it automates.

<<research context step>>=
# Set the research context

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+llm\s+context\s+llm-overview\s+["']
  - "Research context updated."
  - "Research context:"
hint: "The context verb takes the session and a quoted question; run it again without the question to read it back."
```

Tell the model what the review is about:

```
scholar llm context llm-overview "Which tools and practices make literate programming usable in practice?"
scholar llm context llm-overview
```

The second command, without a question, reads the context back.

The research context is stored in the session. Every LLM command on
this session---classification and synthesis---puts it in front of the
model, so write it as the question you actually want answered, not as
a list of keywords.
@

\section{Step 6: Preview the classification prompt}

This is the first half of the tutorial's central contrast.
[[--dry-run]] makes the classify command print the prompt it would send
and stop, so the learner reads exactly what the model will be asked:
the research context from step~5 and the tag vocabulary the model must
stay within.
The required patterns anchor on Scholar's dry-run banner and on the
context heading inside the prompt, which ties the preview back to the
value the learner just set.

[[--no-examples]] is given because a fresh session has no human
decisions to learn from; without the flag Scholar would warn about the
missing examples and proceed anyway, and the warning would distract from
the prompt.
The three [[-t]] tags are the two-sided vocabulary that the
agent-facing tutorial (\cref{ch:tutorial-scholar-with-an-agent}) relies
on; seeding them here shows in the prompt as an explicit allowed list,
which is the point: the learner sees that the model cannot invent tags.

<<dry run classify step>>=
# Preview the classification prompt

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+llm\s+classify\s+llm-overview\s+.*--dry-run\b
  - "Prompt (dry run):"
  - "## Research Context"
hint: "Add --dry-run to the classify command to print the prompt instead of sending it."
```

Before spending a model call, look at what the model would be asked:

```
scholar llm classify llm-overview --no-examples --dry-run \
    -t supports-claim -t adjacent-subtopic -t off-topic-false-hit
```

`--dry-run` prints the prompt and stops. Find your research context in
it, and the list of allowed tags: the model must pick from that list
and cannot invent tags of its own.

`--no-examples` skips the training examples, because a new session has
no human decisions to learn from yet. The `-t` flags seed the tag
vocabulary; tags already used in the session are always included.
@

\section{Step 7: Classify for real}

The second half of the contrast: the same command without
[[--dry-run]].
Now the model answers, and Scholar records one decision per paper with
a confidence.
The step asks for the status table straight after, because the status
table is where the tutorial's rule becomes visible: the new rows count
as \emph{LLM (unreviewed)}, not as decisions.
The required patterns anchor on Scholar's summary line and on that
status row.

The step text says before the command that it spends a model call.
The tutorial has been free up to here, and a learner should never
discover a cost afterwards.

<<classify step>>=
# Classify for real

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: Classified \d+ papers
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+llm\s+status\s+llm-overview\b
  - "LLM (unreviewed)"
hint: "Run the classify command without --dry-run, then look at the session with the status verb."
```

This step makes a real model call (five short papers, so a small one).

Run the classification without the dry-run flag:

```
scholar llm classify llm-overview --no-examples \
    -t supports-claim -t adjacent-subtopic -t off-topic-false-hit
```

Then show where the session stands:

```
scholar llm status llm-overview
```

The papers the model classified appear as *LLM (unreviewed)*. Each
carries the model's confidence, but none is a decision yet: the model
proposed, and someone still has to dispose.
@

\section{Step 8: Confirm one decision as a human}

Here the paper stays the same and the decider changes.
The learner records a keep on a paper the model already kept, with the
non-interactive [[sessions decide]] command, and the status table moves
that row from \emph{LLM (unreviewed)} to \emph{LLM (reviewed)}.
[[sessions decide]] is used rather than the TUI because it keeps this
tutorial free of screen-driven steps and because it is the form an
agent would use; the review tutorial
(\cref{ch:tutorial-reviewing-in-the-tui}) covers the keys that do the
same thing interactively.
The required patterns anchor on the decide command with a [[--doi]]
selector, on Scholar's confirmation line and on the reviewed row.
The decide command needs a DOI, and [[sessions show]] prints one under
every title as the paper id ([[doi:...]]), which is also what
[[--paper-id]] accepts; the step has the learner read the listing first,
so the identifier is met where it is later needed.

The step text also states the iteration rule: a reviewed decision the
learner \emph{changed} becomes a training example for the next
classification round, which is why the next round can drop
[[--no-examples]].
That closes the loop the README describes, and the learner has now
produced each of its parts by hand.

<<confirm decision step>>=
# Confirm one decision as a human

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+sessions\s+decide\s+llm-overview\s+--keep\s+--doi\s+\S+
  - "marked kept"
  - "LLM (reviewed)"
hint: "Pick a DOI from the session, record a keep for it with the decide verb, then check the status table again."
```

Find a paper the model kept and its DOI: `sessions show` prints each
paper's id under its title, `doi:` followed by the DOI.

```
scholar sessions show llm-overview
```

Record the same keep yourself, filling in that DOI (without the `doi:`
prefix; `--paper-id` takes the whole id instead):

```
scholar sessions decide llm-overview --keep --doi <the DOI> --tag supports-claim
```

Then run `scholar llm status llm-overview` again.

The paper has moved from *LLM (unreviewed)* to *LLM (reviewed)*: the
model's proposal is now your decision. A reviewed decision that you
changed (a discard where the model kept, or the reverse) becomes a
training example, so the next `scholar llm classify` on this session
can drop `--no-examples` and learn from your corrections. In the
review TUI, the keys `K` and `d` on a queued paper do exactly this.
@

\section{Step 9: Preview the synthesis prompt}

Synthesis repeats the dry-run contrast at the second paid stage, so the
learner meets the pattern twice and can generalise it: any [[scholar
llm]] command that would call a model accepts [[--dry-run]].
The preview also shows the two preconditions in action.
The synthesis needs a research context (set in step~5) and at least one
kept paper (there are some after steps 7--8); with either missing,
Scholar refuses instead of writing prose about nothing.
The required patterns anchor on the dry-run banner and on the research
question heading inside the prompt, so the learner sees the same
context from step~5 reappear in a different role: as the question the
synthesis answers rather than the criterion it classifies by.

<<dry run synthesis step>>=
# Preview the synthesis prompt

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+llm\s+synthesize\s+llm-overview\s+.*--dry-run\b
  - "Prompt (dry run):"
  - "## Research Question"
hint: "The synthesize verb takes --dry-run too; the prompt shows the research question and the kept papers."
```

Preview the synthesis the same way:

```
scholar llm synthesize llm-overview --dry-run
```

The prompt opens with your research question and lists the kept papers
with their abstracts. When the kept papers carry tags, the synthesis is
organised by them, and the preview shows one prompt per theme.

Synthesis needs two things, and refuses without them: a research
context, and at least one kept paper. This session has both.
@

\section{Step 10: Write the literature overview}

The last paid stage.
The command writes the overview to a file, and the step asks the
learner to look at its head, because the structure is the point: the
output always starts with a \emph{Research Question} section that
carries the provenance (the query and provider pairs, the timestamp, the
review statistics), then the synthesis, a conclusion and a
deterministic reference list.
The required patterns anchor on Scholar's confirmation line and on that
first heading.

Markdown is the default because it is readable as-is.
The step text contrasts it with [[-f latex]] in prose only, so the
learner knows the LaTeX output exists without having to compile it: a
standalone document embeds its own BibTeX file and needs [[biber]],
while [[--no-standalone]] gives a fragment for [[\input]] with the
bibliography written next to it.
Finally the text names [[scholar rq]], which starts from a research
question and runs steps 4--7 in one go; it is mentioned last so that the
learner meets the shortcut only after having done each of its parts.

<<synthesize step>>=
# Write the literature overview

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+llm\s+synthesize\s+llm-overview\s+.*-o\s+overview\.md\b
  - "Synthesis written to"
  - "Research Question"
hint: "Run the synthesize verb with -o to write a Markdown file, then look at the top of that file."
```

This step makes a real model call.

Write the overview to a file:

```
scholar llm synthesize llm-overview -o overview.md
```

Then read the beginning of it with `head -20 overview.md`.

The overview always opens with a *Research Question* section carrying
the provenance: which queries ran on which providers, when, and the
review statistics. The synthesis follows, organised by theme when the
kept papers carry tags, then a conclusion and a reference list.

Markdown is the default. With `-f latex` you get a compilable document
that embeds its own BibTeX file (compile it with biber), and with
`-f latex --no-standalone -o part.tex` a fragment for `\input` with the
bibliography written next to it.

If you start from a research question rather than a query, `scholar rq
"..."` does steps 4 to 7 in one go: it generates provider-specific
queries, runs them, stores the question as the research context and
classifies the hits.
@

\section{Step 11: Which commands spend a call?}

The closing question is the fusion step.
Having varied one flag at a time, the learner is now asked to sort the
whole [[scholar llm]] surface along the axis the tutorial was about:
which commands spend a model call.
The options mix the free commands ([[context]], [[status]]) with the
paid ones ([[classify]], [[synthesize]]) and add [[scholar rq]] and a
[[--dry-run]] variant as the two cases most likely to be misjudged: the
former because it was only mentioned in prose, the latter because it
looks like the paid command it previews.
A [[multi_select]] with literal option text is used so the answer is
checked against the commands, not against their position.

<<paid calls question step>>=
# Which commands spend a call?

```tutorial-step
kind: multi_select
options:
  - scholar llm context
  - scholar llm classify
  - scholar llm classify --dry-run
  - scholar llm status
  - scholar llm synthesize
  - scholar rq
answers:
  - scholar llm classify
  - scholar llm synthesize
  - scholar rq
hint: "A command spends a call only when a model actually answers; previews, settings and status tables do not."
```

Pick every command in this list that makes a model call.

Think back over what you ran: which commands returned immediately with
something Scholar already had, and which ones waited for a model?
@
